# Recursion and Divide-and-Conquer: Solve Big Problems with Small Ones

> A recursive function solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller
> copy of the same problem. Divide-and-conquer builds whole algorithms
> on that move: split, solve, combine — and logarithms do the rest.

## 1. Recursion: A Function That Calls Itself

Every recursive definition has exactly two ingredients:

1. **Base case** — a small input answered directly, with no self-call.
2. **Recursive case** — the problem restated in terms of smaller input.

```text
FACTORIAL(n):
  if n <= 1:
    return 1
  return n * FACTORIAL(n - 1)
```

FACTORIAL(4) unwinds down to FACTORIAL(1), then multiplies back up.

[warning]
A missing or unreachable base case means the calls never bottom out —
each call pushes a frame until the program dies with a **stack
overflow**.
[/warning]

## 2. The Call Stack

Each call pushes a **stack frame**; each return pops one frame.

| Call | Waits on | Returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| FACTORIAL(3) | FACTORIAL(2) | 6 |
| FACTORIAL(2) | FACTORIAL(1) | 2 |
| FACTORIAL(1) | — base case | 1 |

[info title="Space is part of the cost"]
An O(n)-time recursion still consumes O(n) stack space; a loop uses O(1).
[/info]

## 3. Four Classic Recursions

| Problem | Recurrence | Cost | Note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Factorial | f(n) = n × f(n−1) | O(n) | one call per level |
| Fibonacci | f(n) = f(n−1) + f(n−2) | O(2ⁿ) naive, O(n) memoized | overlapping calls |
| Tower of Hanoi | h(n) = 2·h(n−1) + 1 | 2ⁿ − 1 moves | exponential is optimal |
| Euclid's GCD | gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b) | O(log min(a, b)) | multiplicative shrink |

[note]
Tower of Hanoi with 64 discs takes 2⁶⁴ − 1 moves — about 585 billion
years at one per second. Exponential growth is a wall.
[/note]

## 4. Overlapping Subproblems: The Fibonacci Trap

The naive call tree recomputes the same values again and again:

@diagram tree id="fibonacci-call-tree" layout="tree" aria="Naive Fibonacci call tree where fib of 3 and fib of 2 are computed repeatedly"
  "fib(5)" -> "fib(4)"
  "fib(5)" -> "fib(3)"
  "fib(4)" -> "fib(3)"
  "fib(4)" -> "fib(2)"
  "fib(3)" -> "fib(2)"
  "fib(3)" -> "fib(1)"
@/diagram

fib(3) is computed twice and fib(2) three times; the branching roughly
doubles every level, so naive Fibonacci costs about O(2ⁿ).

[definition]
**Memoization** records each answer at first computation; repeats become
lookups. Fibonacci drops from O(2ⁿ) to O(n) time for O(n) memory.
[/definition]

## 5. Memoization vs Tabulation

| Aspect | Memoization (top-down) | Tabulation (bottom-up) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Style | recursion plus a cache | fill a table with loops |
| Computes | only the states actually needed | every state up to n |
| Risk | deep call stack | must order the table correctly |
| Best at | sparse state spaces | dense, regular ones |

Both compute each state once — differing only in direction.

## 6. Divide-and-Conquer: Divide, Conquer, Combine

Divide-and-conquer cuts the input in half and recurses on each piece:

1. **Divide** the problem into smaller instances of itself.
2. **Conquer** each piece recursively; tiny pieces are solved directly.
3. **Combine** the answers into the final result.

Halving is powerful: log₂ n levels separate a million items from one.

| Problem | Divide | Combine | Total |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Binary search | keep one half | nothing to merge | O(log n) |
| Merge sort | two halves | linear merge | O(n log n) |
| Max subarray | two halves | best run crossing the middle | O(n log n) |

## 7. Binary Search as Halving in Action

Looking for one value among 1,000,000 sorted items:

| After step | Items still possible |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | 1,000,000 |
| 10 | about 977 |
| 20 | about 1 |

Twenty comparisons, because log₂(1,000,000) ≈ 20: each comparison
discards half the range, and that is the entire trick.

## 8. Recursion vs Iteration

| Aspect | Recursion | Iteration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Readability | mirrors the problem's shape | can be long-winded |
| Memory | O(depth) stack frames | O(1) |
| Failure mode | stack overflow | infinite loop |
| Best for | trees, divide-and-conquer | counting, scans |

[note]
**Tail recursion** — the self-call is the last action, nothing pending
afterwards — can be compiled into a loop; Python does not, so deep
recursion there must become a loop with an explicit stack.
[/note]

[summary]
Recursion needs a base case plus a recursive case, and spends one
stack frame per level. Overlapping subproblems make naive recursion
exponential — memoize or tabulate. Divide-and-conquer halves problems:
binary search O(log n), merge sort O(n log n).
[/summary]
